00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:09.830 align:start position:0%
 
oh my god  hey out there everyone welcome in  welcome welcome to another edition of  market takes i am your host dion robon  reporter at the wall street journal  thank you so much for being with us i  know you could have been anywhere in the  world but you're here with me and i  appreciate that look today we are diving  deep jumping all the way into today's  u.s retail sales report for july we are  going to be unpacking that report and  talking all about the latest big piece  of data on the u.s economy we're going  to be talking about the headline number  on that retail sales report how that  report stacked up to expectations uh  really how the squares with the new  confidence that we're seeing on wall  street kind of a clot across asset  classes about this new expected soft  landing for the u.s economy that was  very much in doubt just a month or two  ago back in june now it seems like  markets are pricing in that soft landing  which means continued economic growth  which means lower inflation which means  the fed really is likely to be able to  accomplish what they're trying to do  which is raise rates to curb inflation  while also  not seeing a big pickup in unemployment  or a crash in the economy that would be  a hard landing uh we're going to talk  about how today's report could affect  the market and what it tells us about  the third quarter and what it tells us  about the fourth quarter uh going  forward as we as we look forward and  don't look back maybe we're done with  all that so listen let's jump right into  it the headline number on this july  retail sales report zero percent uh  month over month gain on retail sales um  that is flat versus expectations for a  0.2 percent gain so we missed  expectations but only by a little bit  and we're up 10  from where we were in july 2021 so  retail sales continuing to look strong  uh folks continuing to go out and spend  and buy but  the devil is in the details on this  report so we're going to get into all  those listen weakness was expected  coming in we weren't expecting a big  blowout number on this retail sales  report and we certainly obviously didn't  get it right zero percent uh change but  remember in july inflation was always  also 0.0 percent from the previous month  so we got a reading that's right in line  with that cpi inflation report that we  got for july as well uh this is a big  step down from the one percent gain that  we saw in june but that number was also  revised lower today so june being  revised from one percent gain to zero  point eight percent gain uh so you're  just seeing kind of a general come down  on the retail sales number it's it's not  falling off a cliff it's not dropping  into negative territory but you're  seeing a kind of stall out and if you  look at the overall trend year over year  you're seeing this kind of flattening  trend very clearly the report is showing  retail sales are kind of flattening look  the big thing about this report was the  major drag was gasoline if you take  gasoline sales gas prices out of this  well the numbers look a lot stronger  and that again matches up with what we  saw for cpi for july sales excluding  autos and gas were up 0.7  that beat expectations  and you also saw gasoline stations were  the biggest drawdown the biggest uh  thing pulling the report back gasoline  station sales were down about two  percent this month um worse than  expectations motor vehicles and parts  were next  down about 1.6  so really what that tells you is the  declines that we're seeing the things  really dragging back and pulling back  retail sales are the auto sector the  gasoline sector all things related to  that those are the things that have  really taken off and boomed over the  course of much of this year or over the  course of much of last year that we're  seeing really kind of moderate economy  is generally holding up so that's not  necessarily a bad thing that's not  something that people should be worried  about and in fact if this trend  continues if gas prices continue to come  down  that's going to be a headwind or sorry  it's going to be a tailwind for the  economy for the rest of the year  the other big piece of this was that  retail sales the control group right the  control group was stronger than  anticipated that excludes food services  automobile dealerships building  materials and gasoline stations so that  number was up about almost one percent  and so again continuing if you take gas  out of this out of the equation you're  seeing continued pickup in sales you're  seeing continued strength from the  consumer and these are all good things  as we look out at the economy nine of  the 13 pieces of this retail sales  report were higher on the month of the  best was non-store retailers that got a  big boost from amazon prime day which  happened in july uh that's definitely  something that folks you know you wanna  you wanna write that out that's one of  these kind of  exogenous events it's not something you  can count on each month so we're  interesting to see how non-store  retailers can perform as we move  throughout the year  non-store retailers up about 20 percent  from july 2021 so that again is a  category that has showed continued  strength for a while  over the course of the year now  let's put this retail sales report in  context right nothing nothing exists in  a vacuum in a bubble alone unto itself  we are seeing the housing market in  recession so  the national association of home  builders came out earlier this week and  told us we're seeing the housing market  in recession you had a reading of 49  below that marker of 50 which you know  is the difference between expansion and  contraction you had a 49 reading on the  nahb  home building sentiment index  earlier this week that tells us the  housing market which we've known for a  while right we've seen consistent  declines in existing home sales in new  home sales and pending home sales and  we've continued to see that  and so we're now seeing what nahb calls  a housing recession that's going to have  some knock-on effects throughout the  economy and something you want to keep  your eye on not just for what it says  about the housing market folks buying a  house but for what it does to rents  owner equivalent rents cpi all those  things moving forward  the empire manufacturing service that's  basically the new york state region the  federal reserve the the country is is  divided into about 12 regions by the  federal reserve one of those is the  empire or the new york state region that  was down  31.3 a negative 31.3 reading in august  that's a 42 point decline from the  previous month that is a huge drop off  the second largest decline in history  normally those fed regional  manufacturing surveys don't make a big  dent on impre on investor screens this  is definitely one to watch that happened  earlier this week so you might start to  see a a an increased pickup in the  decline in some of those manufacturing  numbers definitely something to look out  for like i talked about housing again  housing starts were down 10 in july  single-family housing was down 18.5  multi-family housing up 18  what does that tell you it tells you  individuals families not going out and  buying houses developers investors uh  those big money private equity funds and  things like that they're continuing to  buy and continuing to invest in the  housing market we'll see how that winds  up we also saw another decline in  mortgage applications  just this morning from the mortgage  brokers association the mortgage bankers  association excuse me uh so again we're  seeing more weakness in the housing  market and that could have more of these  knock-on effects  housing spending  it's one of those things that doesn't  just impact housing prices like i said  you see this in retail and home  furnishings you see this in places like  target walmart lowe's home depot because  if you're not buying a new house well  you're not buying things to furnish that  house or to make improvements or repairs  on that house and so that kind of can  have a snowball effect throughout the  economy general merchandise sales and  clothing sales were both were the next  two biggest lagers on the month third  and fourth third and fourth weakest  categories that we saw  for this retail sales report so again  something to watch out for as we look  forward  remember though as we saw as we're  seeing this decline in housing decline  in gas prices we are seeing a decline in  cpi and ppi the consumer price index  producer price index that's what  consumers are paying you and i we go to  the store as well as what those  businesses are paying when they buy  those goods to sell them to us that ppi  report producer prices has been a bit of  a harbinger of what's to come and so you  can really start to expect there's  reason to have optimism that inflation  could be starting to see a secular  decline here and we could consistently  start to move lower that's what you want  to see you want to see those price  increases slowing down on a consistent  basis moving forward  and we're seeing more signs of that so  price is flat that's telling us this  retail sales report that u.s consumers  are keeping up with the overall trend in  inflation not working against what the  federal reserve is doing raising rates  and making things more expensive in the  economy so we have some consistency  there um one thing that i wanted to  highlight was a report from james  knightley he is the chief international  economist at ing he had a really  interesting report that came out last  night i want to read a couple short  passages from that because he's worried  okay q3 looks like it's going to hold up  we're going to see strong economic  growth in the third quarter in contrast  to that weak growth that we've seen  through the first and second quarter but  what about the fourth quarter well he  says we strongly suspect consumer  spending will be lifted by the cash flow  boost caused by the plunge in gasoline  prices and decent employment gains trade  will be supportive too  inventory is less of a drag and now that  we know that manufacturing is rebounding  putting this all together he's expecting  three percent growth in the third  quarter so  versus the third quarter of 2021 they  say that's firmly in the cards the worry  is what happens in the fourth quarter  and he says we're closely looking to see  if this decline that we saw in the  empire state report which again the  second biggest drop on record is  replicated in the philadelphia federal  reserve report we get that thursday and  the richmond fed report will get that  wednesday as well as others later this  month uh even if it's an aberration  there are reasons to expect weaker  activity as we move forward into the  fourth quarter you've got a china  slowdown a likely recession in europe  and those are going to weigh heavily on  the global economy which again  reverberates into the us and is  something that we really can't get out  of and of course he points out the  housing market he says it will also act  as a major headwind definitely something  to keep in mind look let's check out the  the market right now and the market  reaction we're seeing broadly negative  you've got the nasdaq down about one  percent the smp down about half of that  and i'm sorry the dow down about half  that and the s p down by about 0.7  percent uh let's look at the bond market  because i think that's going to really  tell us what we need to be looking at  you're seeing yields bond yields up kind  of across the board uh more pronounced  at the shorter end of the curve than the  longer end of the curve which basically  tells us that the market is not pricing  in more inflation that you're seeing  that long-term inflationary outlook kind  of brought in just a little bit as  yields rise and bond prices fall kind of  across the yield curve um as we look out  at currencies right now you're seeing  euro up you're seeing the dollar up  against the yen  you're seeing the swiss franc canadian  dollar  a lot of these things rising right now  the overall thing that we've seen is  dollar strength coming off the board  over the past couple weeks we're seeing  the dollar lower it is a little bit  higher today it's up about 0.2 percent  even as the euro is up so that tells you  that against other currencies the dollar  is moving higher um a big reason that  we're seeing i think a lot of that  weakness in the stock market that we're  seeing right now and the the moves in  the bond market  is this uk inflation report that we got  this morning we got a couple different  reports on inflation from the united  kingdom the their version of the cpi the  consumer price index up 10.1 percent  year over year that was stronger than  expected and a new multi-decade high  over there in the uk uh uk producer  prices their ppi and cpi come out on the  same day that rose 1.6 percent of the  month and 17  on the year that was above expectations  also in the market we're seeing target  having a much weaker than expected  quarter prices or their profits declined  by about 90 percent as it slash prices  to get rid of a glut of inventory we've  known for a while that inventories have  been a problem however target maintained  its outlook for the whole year so again  that's a good sign for the long term  right now the short term not looking so  great so look let's step back let's take  a step back what did we learn today from  this retail sales report well we learned  that consumer buying has continued we  learned that gasoline is coming down  that sales of gas are dragging down the  overall increases that we saw if you  strip out gasoline if you strip out the  auto sector two things that boomed and  shot up very high in price  last year and early into this year well  those are coming down prices are coming  down there and overall as you look out  at the retail sales landscape consumers  still buying still going out spending  money you're not seeing a significant  pullback except in those gas and auto  sectors  that's a big thing  we know that the market did not like the  jobs report which was very strong for  july  we're seeing really a continuation of  trends inflation coming down uh jobs  continue to be strong and consumers  continue to spend as gas prices go down  this is probably not going to change the  market's mind right now about the  bullish theme of a soft landing the  retail sales report today really tells  us all right you know what not too hot  not too cold this is just about right it  doesn't change the narrative even though  we are seeing some weakness in the stock  market right now  you could expect that to reverse course  because we did not see a break in the  trend from this report so again that's  what's holding up here's what to look  out for next today later today at 2 pm  eastern time  right here we are going to get the fomc  minutes the minutes from the fed's  latest meeting or from its july meeting  uh that's the the the word that we're  getting on you know the the official  minutes from that meeting we'll get  those later today at 2pm uh markets eyes  will be very closely watching that and  that could tell us a lot about what's to  come for september what their  expectations are again the fed has kind  of moved from forward guidance to data  dependent saying hey what the data tells  us that's how we're going to react the  data we got today didn't tell us that  much has changed on the overall theme uh  we get the producer con producer i'm  sorry personal consumption expenditures  report that's the fed's favorite measure  of inflation that's coming out next week  and we will also get the university of  michigan's consumer sentiment report  next week on friday we are going to be  doing another live stream on that so  please tune in make sure you don't miss  that that's going to be a big report  because consumer confidence consumer  sentiment is the next big frontier that  could change the trajectory of the  market or really reinforce the bullish  soft landing narrative that we've got  right now so we'll be unpacking all that  next week uh listen that's going to do  it for us here on market takes i  appreciate you joining me i am dion  rabone reporter for the wall street  journal thank you for being with me on  this edition and join us next week when  we unpack the consumer confidence index  or consumer sentiment index from the  university of michigan next friday all  right thank you for joining us we'll be  back next week i hope to see you there

